# Project Guide

This project is an ASP.NET Core (.NET 8) backend with a React/TypeScript/Tailwind frontend. The built frontend is **served as static files** by the backend.

## Stack

- **Backend:** .NET 8, ASP.NET Core Minimal APIs — serves the built frontend (static files) and API. Includes PDF text extraction (`PdfPig`), Microsoft Excel workbook generation (in-memory via `ClosedXML`), Microsoft Graph upload to OneDrive, and Claude inference via the Dibbla AI Gateway.
- **Frontend:** React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS v4, Vite — build output goes to `dist/` at repo root.
- **Deploy:** Multi-stage Docker on the .NET ASP.NET runtime image, default port 80.

## Project layout

- `Program.cs` — ASP.NET Core app: API under `/api/*`, static-file serving + SPA fallback at `/`, and the port binding that writes the `.dev` port files. Calls Claude via the Dibbla AI Gateway (`DIBBLA_AI_GATEWAY_URL`) for expense extraction.
- `PlatformClient.cs` — AI Gateway (Claude) calls + Microsoft Graph token vending. `ExcelReportBuilder.cs` — ClosedXML workbook + OneDrive upload. `PdfTextExtractor.cs` — PdfPig text extraction. `SessionStore.cs` — in-memory sessions + cleanup. `Models.cs` — `UploadedFile`/`Session`/`Expense`.
- `frontend/` — Vite app; `vite.config.ts` sets `outDir: "../dist"`. Dev server proxies `/api` to backend.
- `dist/` — Generated by `npm run build` in `frontend/`; do not edit by hand. The backend serves this directory as static files.
- `dibbla-task.yaml` — Dibbla task definition (env, tools, build steps, dev servers).
- `Dockerfile` — Multi-stage: build frontend → `dotnet publish` the backend (then copy `dist`) → .NET ASP.NET runtime image.
- `APP.md` — App store page: short description of what the app does. Must exist and be kept up-to-date when the app changes.

## Conventions

- **.NET:** Uses `DotNetEnv` for optional `.env` (loaded without clobbering — real environment variables always win, so a committed dev `.env` never overrides production config). AI inference goes directly to the Dibbla AI Gateway: `DIBBLA_AI_GATEWAY_URL` defaults to `https://ai.dibbla.net`. `DIBBLA_ALIAS` is sent as the `X-Dibbla-App` header for per-app attribution in the gateway ledger — **must be passed explicitly on deploy** (see Deployment below); without it calls show up as `source=external`. `GATEWAY_URL` defaults to `https://api.dibbla.net` and is only used for the Microsoft OAuth handshake; `PORT` defaults to 80 (8210 in dev).
- **Microsoft Graph token vending (gateway contract):** `GetMicrosoftTokenAsync` (`PlatformClient.cs`) validates the Dibbla token, then `GET {GATEWAY_URL}/auth/oauth/microsoft/token?user_id=<id>&scopes=Files.ReadWrite.All&return_to=<url>`. This is a **public** gateway route (identity is the `user_id` query param — do **not** add an `Authorization`/`X-User-ID` header). It returns `200 {access_token}`, or `403 {error, message, missing_scopes, granted_scopes, login_url}` when the user has no Microsoft connection / hasn't granted the scope — the frontend then redirects to `login_url` for consent. The endpoint mirrors the Google one (`/auth/oauth/google/token`) and lives in the gateway's `auth-service` (`handlers/auth.go` `MicrosoftAccessToken`, registered in `handlers/router.go`); Microsoft login must request `offline_access` so a refresh token is stored. The token request logs each step under the `[msauth]` prefix for debugging.
- **API:** REST under `/api/` — `POST /api/upload`, `GET /api/uploads`, `DELETE /api/uploads/:id`, `GET /api/check-scopes` (verifies Microsoft `Files.ReadWrite.All` scope), `POST /api/generate-report` (returns `{workbook_url, expenses}`). Add new routes in `Program.cs` before the static-file/SPA-fallback mapping.
- **Frontend:** Use Tailwind v4 with `@theme` in `frontend/src/index.css`. Prefer design tokens: `dibbla-green`, `dibbla-dark`, `dibbla-card`.
- **Static/SPA:** Keep the existing order: `UseStaticFiles` serving `dist/`, then a `MapFallback` to `index.html` for client-side routing.
- **APP.md:** Ensure `APP.md` exists in the repo and is kept up-to-date with the latest description of what the app does. It is used as the app store page for the app; update it whenever features or purpose change.

## Frontend architecture — avoid large inline blobs

- **SVGs & logos:** Keep large SVG assets in their own files under `frontend/src/components/` (e.g. `DibblaLogo.tsx`) or `frontend/src/assets/` (e.g. `logo.svg`). Import them — never paste multi-hundred-line SVG paths inline into page components.
- **Reusable layout shell:** The background scaffolding (grid pattern, blur accents, scrollbar styles in `index.css`) is shared across pages. When rebuilding a page, only replace the *content* inside the `z-10` container — don't regenerate the backdrop boilerplate.
- **Dibbla DB connection string:** The format is `postgres://<DB_CONNECTION>/<db_name>?sslmode=require` where `DB_CONNECTION` comes from `dibbla secrets get DB_CONNECTION`. No need to manually assemble — just slot the secret and DB name into this template.

## Workflow

- **Local run:** `cd frontend && npm install && npm run build && cd ..` then `dotnet run` (or use Dibbla task).
- **Dev:** Backend on 8210, Vite dev server on 5305 with API proxy to backend.
- **Docker:** `docker build -t expense-reporter-excel-template-1 .` — the image builds the frontend and `dotnet publish`es the backend in separate stages, so a local `dist/` is not required.

## Dev servers & ports (IMPORTANT for local runs)

Port handling is self-healing so non-technical users can start the app without hitting port conflicts:

- **Backend publishes its actual port.** On startup, the backend writes the bound port and PID to `.dev/backend.port` and `.dev/backend.pid`. These files are cleaned up on shutdown (SIGINT/SIGTERM). If the preferred port (8210) is taken, the backend falls back to 8211, 8212, … up to 20 tries.
- **Vite proxy reads `.dev/backend.port` dynamically.** `frontend/vite.config.ts` uses a `router` callback so `/api` always forwards to the backend's real port, even if it fell back. `strictPort: true` on Vite means the dev server fails loudly if 5305 is taken (no silent switching).
- **Pre-flight in `dibbla-task.yaml` (`reclaim-ports` step).** Before starting servers, any stale backend (`ExpenseReporter`) or `vite` process from a previous run is killed. Ports held by unrelated processes cause a clear error — never auto-killed.
- **Do not hardcode `8210` on the frontend.** Use relative `/api/...` paths (Vite proxies them). If you need the backend URL in a script, read `.dev/backend.port`.
- **To stop cleanly:** rerun the dibbla task (pre-flight reclaims stale processes), or manually `kill $(cat .dev/backend.pid)`.

When changing the frontend, rebuild (`npm run build` in `frontend/`) before running or building the backend so `dist/` is up to date.

## Databases

If the user asks to add a database:

- **ORM:** Use Entity Framework Core (`Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore` + `Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL`). Register the `DbContext` in `Program.cs` using a connection string from the environment.
- **Connection string:** Do not hardcode. Read from an env var (e.g. `DATABASE_URL` or `DB_CONNECTION`). To get the value from Dibbla, use the **Dibbla CLI skill** and run e.g. `dibbla secrets get DB_CONNECTION` if a secret with that name exists.
- **Create and manage databases via Dibbla only:** Do not add custom SQL or Postgres provisioning code. Use the Dibbla CLI so the app's database gets the right setup (including pgvector). First check existing databases to avoid name conflicts: `dibbla db list`. Create with `dibbla db create <db_name>`. **Database names** must match `^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,62}$` (lowercase, digits, underscores only; no hyphens — use e.g. `garden_demo` not `garden-demo`). See `.claude/skills/dibbla/SKILL.md` and `dibbla --skill-prompt` for full docs.
- **Local dev:** Use the same env var in `.env` (e.g. a local Postgres URL). Document the variable in `.env.example` if needed.

## Deployment

**Do not deploy unless the user explicitly asks.** After making changes, assume the user wants to test locally first. Point them to the local dev servers (backend on port 8210, Vite on port 5305) and remind them to hard-reload the browser (Cmd+Shift+R / Ctrl+Shift+R) to bypass the cache if frontend changes aren't showing.

When the user asks to deploy, use the **Dibbla CLI skill** (see `.claude/skills/dibbla/SKILL.md`):

- Deploy with `dibbla deploy .` (no `--yes` needed). Use `--force` to overwrite an existing deployment with the same alias.
- **Always pass `--require-login`** so the app requires authentication. This app uses the authenticated user's identity to obtain Microsoft Graph OAuth tokens via the Dibbla gateway.
- **Always pass `--microsoft-scopes Files.ReadWrite.All`** so the user is prompted to grant OneDrive / Excel access on login. Without this scope the app cannot create or upload expense report workbooks.
- **Always pass `-e DIBBLA_ALIAS=<same value as --alias>`** so the backend can stamp `X-Dibbla-App` on AI Gateway calls. Without this the call shows up as `source=external` in the `ai.dibbla.net/console` ledger instead of being attributed to the deployed app.
- If the app listens on a port other than 80, set it with `--port <port>` (match the port in the Dockerfile).
- For updates to an existing deployment, use `dibbla update`; run `dibbla --skill-prompt` for full options.
- **Example deploy command:**
  ```
  dibbla deploy . --alias <name> --require-login --microsoft-scopes Files.ReadWrite.All -e DIBBLA_ALIAS=<name>
  ```
- **If the `--microsoft-scopes` CLI flag is not yet available** in your installed dibbla version, deploy without it, then set Microsoft scopes (`Files.ReadWrite.All`) on the deployed app via the deploy-ui User Management dialog. The app's `/api/check-scopes` will redirect the user to grant access on next load.

## Docker & Dibbla build (lessons learned)

- **Dockerfile uses `dotnet publish`:** Restore against `ExpenseReporter.csproj`, copy `*.cs` + `appsettings.json`, then `dotnet publish -c Release -o /publish` and copy the output into the ASP.NET runtime image. The frontend `dist/` is copied from the node stage to sit next to the published DLL (the backend serves it).
- **Target framework alignment:** The `<TargetFramework>` in `ExpenseReporter.csproj` (`net8.0`) must match the SDK/runtime image tags (`sdk:8.0`, `aspnet:8.0`). Bumping one means bumping the others.
- **`.env` must not ship to production:** It's listed in `.dockerignore`, and the backend loads `.env` without clobbering real env vars — so even if it leaked, Dibbla-injected config (PORT, DIBBLA_ALIAS, …) would still win.
- **Local Vite proxy:** The frontend proxies `/api` to the backend port from `.dev/backend.port` (or `PORT` env, default 8210). The dibbla task sets `PORT=8210` and the proxy self-heals if the backend falls back. When running the backend manually, use `PORT=8210 dotnet run`.

## Maintaining rules and skills

When you learn something worthwhile remembering for future sessions, update the project's guidance:

- **Dibbla CLI skill** — installed automatically via `npx skills add dibbla-agents/skills --skill dibbla`. Do not edit these files locally; they are managed by skills.sh.
- **Project-specific knowledge** (this repo's stack, layout, conventions, Docker/.NET quirks, etc.) → **`CLAUDE.md`** and **`.cursorrules`** (keep both in sync)

Keep both files accurate and up-to-date so the next session can benefit from what was learned.
